WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.090
Marvel's most powerful superhero has finally
arrived on the big screen, leaving absolutely

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:10.060
no doubt that Carol Danvers will go on to
play a vital role in upcoming MCU storylines.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.520
And like any action-packed Marvel fest, there
were plenty of references onscreen that were

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.900
easy to miss amid all the photon blasts and
space battles.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:22.760
From characters with secret futures to sneaky
wardrobe changes to Easter eggs, here are

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.500
all of the small details you missed in Captain
Marvel.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:26.380
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.900
"Oh, spoiler alert."

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.840
One of the film's biggest moments came when
Carol Danvers finally discovered her true

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.100
colors, both figuratively and literally.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:41.160
Upon deciding to ditch the Kree and help the
Skrull refugees, Carol figures she needs a

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.780
new look to match her new mission.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.800
Fortunately, the Kree suit-makers programmed
a Sims wardrobe module into her handy dandy

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.540
wrist gadget, allowing her to switch up her
suit's design right then and there.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.820
She flashes through all the colors of the
rainbow before settling on the iconic blue-red-and

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.080
yellow look that fans of the comics couldn't
help but cheer for.

00:00:59.429 --> 00:01:03.780
But right before her final colors, her suit
rests for a moment on a mild green-and-white

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:07.280
color scheme that might be familiar to die-hard
comics readers.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.530
In Captain Marvel's first comics appearance
as Mar-Vell in Marvel Super-Heroes #12, the

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.200
Kree hero wears a suit with the same colors.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.900
Even if it was only on-screen for a few seconds,
it was a clever nod to the superhero's origins

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.540
and more than a little fitting that it appeared
as a precursor to her current suit design.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.080
Sharp-eyed audience members might have noticed
a strange discrepancy in the way the Kree

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:28.580
were portrayed.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.680
Most of the Kree we see on-screen have blue
skin: Minn-Erva, Att-Lass, Ronan, the list

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:34.360
goes on.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.560
There are only a few exceptions to this that
we see: Carol Danvers, who obviously is human

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:43.980
to begin with, and Yon-Rogg, a true-blue Kree
in every sense but the color.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.400
So what gives?

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.260
Was it a mistake by the filmmakers?

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:51.020
Did Jude Law just decide not to Fünke himself
for production?

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.280
“I feel like a f------ idiot.”

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.200
As it turns out, that was deliberate.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:03.840
Although it's never mentioned in the film,
Captain Marvel #38 explains that when the

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.120
Kree race began their conquest of space, they
mated with other species and created so-called

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.500
"pink-skin Kree," a variant of the species
that looks, conveniently, like Caucasian humans.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.240
Mar-Vell also had this trait, which is one
of the reasons she was able to stay on Earth

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:24.000
as Dr. Lawson and work on her project for
the Skrulls without drawing attention to herself.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:28.000
You know, because people in the office tend
to notice a blue person after the third or

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:28.580
fourth day.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:34.260
As a film, Captain Marvel really starts gaining
momentum when Carol Danvers crashes through

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.940
the roof of a Blockbuster on Earth.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.100
It's a good moment for a laugh, especially
if you happened to live through the mid '90s.

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:44.940
After photon blasting a cardboard cutout of
Arnold Schwarzenegger in a True Lies promo

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:48.560
display, Carol wanders down the aisle, marveling
at the bizarre store.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:50.500
"Welcome to Blockbuster Video."

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:55.080
At one point, she picks up a VHS and examines
it, then puts it back on the shelf.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.780
If you were paying attention, you might have
noticed that the video she looks at is The

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:04.120
Right Stuff, a 1983 film about seven pilots
who train to become the first Americans in

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:04.620
space.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:08.799
It's a fitting film for her to pick up, since
the astronauts in The Right Stuff have an

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.000
arc that mirror's Carol Danvers' own path
through the film.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:16.840
First she discovers her past as an Air Force
pilot, then flies into space herself in a

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.520
modified Air Force craft.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:23.120
There was one thread woven throughout the
plot of Captain Marvel that was never fully

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.600
explained, despite the impact it's had over
the years.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.920
That was Project PEGASUS, the joint Air Force
and NASA project headed by Dr. Lawson.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:34.820
As it turns out, PEGASUS isn't a new creation,
even in the MCU.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.380
Marvel Studios has been working with the idea
since at least 2012.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:43.080
Before The Avengers was released, Marvel put
out a short prelude comic entitled Fury's

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.080
Big Week.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:48.670
The comic connected elements from Captain
America: The First Avenger, The Incredible

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:54.330
Hulk, and Iron Man in preparation for the
big cinematic team-up, and it introduced Project

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:54.980
PEGASUS.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:58.260
Just after that, the Project made its big-screen
debut.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:02.640
If you go back and watch The Avengers closely,
you can see that the opening scene when Loki

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.660
steals the Tesseract goes down at the PEGASUS
facility.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.800
Of course, this isn't entirely the first appearance
for Project PEGASUS.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:12.820
It's actually been around in the comics for
quite awhile.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:17.960
Nominally a research center for powerful energy
sources, the facility has also served as a

00:04:17.960 --> 00:04:20.360
makeshift prison for supervillains over the
years.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:25.520
Notably, Project PEGASUS was once the workplace
of Wendell Vaughn, who became the superhero

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:30.260
Quasar and later took the mantle of Protector
of the Universe after Captain Marvel died.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.280
Yeah, that's as important as it sounds.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:37.000
In one scene toward the end of the film, young
Nick Fury is contemplating the events of the

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.260
past few days and working out a new idea to
find superheroes to protect the Earth.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.560
He reasons that since they just had a run-in
with a whole bunch of aliens, there have to

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:47.560
be more superpowered folk out there.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:52.980
He initially has the name "The Protector Initiative"
on his computer screen before he notices Carol

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:58.000
Danvers' Air Force call sign, Avenger, and
changes it to the name we've all come to love

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.480
over the past decade or so.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:08.360
"There was an idea - Stark knows this - called
the Avengers Initiative."

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:13.620
It's the kind of thing that works as a callback
to past films, and future events, but there's

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.600
actually a second layer to the scene.

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:20.880
There are actually several times that a version
of Captain Marvel has also been designated

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:21.380
"Protector."

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:26.740
One of them is another Kree warrior, Noh-Varr,
who spends some time with the name Captain

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.490
Marvel while working with the Dark Avengers.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:33.160
Eventually, though, Noh-Varr leaves the team
and is given a new name by the Supreme Intelligence:

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:34.020
Protector.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:38.699
More importantly, in one of Mar-Vell's earlier
story arcs, he's transported to the realm

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:43.940
of a being called Eon, who's a giant floating
tree… eyeball thing.

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:48.700
This Eon is also incredibly powerful, and
he leads Captain Marvel on a cosmic journey

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:51.020
that teaches him how to harness all power.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:55.820
At the end of this transformation, Eon gives
him a new title: Protector of the Universe.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:00.560
After that, Mar-Vell uses his powers to help
stop none other than Thanos from destroying

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:01.160
the universe.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:05.620
Considering how close Avengers: Endgame is
to the release of Captain Marvel, it makes

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:09.320
you wonder if the mention is a little more
than just a throwaway joke.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:13.080
We're all expecting to see Captain Marvel
go head to head against the Mad Titan.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:17.880
If there's one character who stole the show
in Captain Marvel, it was definitely Goose,

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:22.140
the unassuming tabby who tagged along with
Danvers and Fury in their adventures.

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:24.120
Who knew Fury was such a cat guy?

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.060
"Aren't you the cutest little thing."

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:30.160
Of course, there's a lot more to this cat
than meets the eye.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:35.500
For starters, the name is clearly a subtle
nod to Top Gun, in which Anthony Edwards'

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.080
character, codenamed "Goose," is an Air Force
pilot.

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:40.780
"HAHAHA.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:41.920
Oh, you kill me.

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:43.060
You really do."

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:48.220
Aside from that, though, there's also the
small detail of the purple people-eating tentacles

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:52.801
that sprout out of Goose's mouth, and the
fact that Goose isn't even a cat at all: he's

00:06:52.801 --> 00:06:56.880
a Flerken, an alien species that just happens
to look like a cat.

00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:01.880
Flerkens first appeared to Marvel comics readers
in 2014 in the pages of Captain Marvel, when

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:06.800
it turned out that Danvers' cat, Chewie, was
secretly one of the tentacle beasts in disguise.

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:11.220
After being attacked by Rocket Raccoon, who
saw Chewie for the Flerken he was, Chewie

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:14.400
finally unleashed the beast and saved Rocket's
life.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.080
Aww, good Flerken.

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:22.960
Young actress Akira Akbar turned in a fine
performance as Monica Rambeau, the 11-year-old

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:26.160
daughter of Carol's friend and fellow pilot
Maria Rambeau.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.640
Monica was there during some of the film's
most hard-hitting moments, helping Carol Danvers

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:35.020
rediscover her past and giving the discovery
that extra emotional edge.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:37.780
She even helped Carol pick the right colors
for her new suit.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:41.520
But one particular line of dialogue carried
more significance than it may have seemed

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:42.520
to.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:45.840
Near the end of the movie, Monica asks if
she'll eventually go into space, too.

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:50.860
That leads to the seemingly casual line: "Only
if you learn to glow like your auntie."

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:54.040
But here's the thing, Monica does learn to
glow like her auntie.

00:07:54.389 --> 00:07:58.580
Her name has changed over the years, but fans
may know her best as Photon, a hero with the

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:00.940
ability to harness electromagnetic forces.

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:05.580
In her current incarnation, Monica Rambeau
goes by the name Spectrum, and there was even

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:08.140
a time when she flew under the Captain Marvel
mantle.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.530
In fact, when Monica's character debuted in
a 1982 issue of The Amazing Spider-Man, she

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:17.220
was billed as the "all new, all-different,
all-terrific Captain Marvel."

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:22.199
Another nod to Monica's comic-book counterpart
can be seen on the side of Maria's fighter,

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.780
which has the stencil "Maria 'Photon' Rambeau."

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:28.280
Does this foreshadow big things in the future
for little Monica?

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:29.500
Maybe, maybe not.

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:32.320
Either way, it's an exciting detail from the
comics.

00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:37.020
Played by Gemma Chan, Minn-Erva is one of
the Starforce members who joins Danvers in

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:39.000
the battle on Torfa early in the film.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:43.690
Minn-Erva has a fairly respectable comics
presence, and she's been involved in some

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:45.400
seriously twisted plots.

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:50.160
She's as Kree as they come, fiercely loyal
to her race and often willing to go to extreme

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.520
lengths for the betterment of the Kree civilization.

00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:57.340
In one particularly heinous plan during the
Civil War event, Minn-Erva tried to release

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:02.100
a biological weapon in California that would
have turned people into mutated monsters.

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:07.320
Captain Marvel was able stop her, even though
Minn-Erva turned into a giant mutation herself

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:08.000
during the fight.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:13.700
In another instance, she was part of the Supreme
Intelligence's plan to destroy 90% of the

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:17.280
Kree population to make the remaining 10%
stronger than ever.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:20.790
Yeah, that's not the kind of person you really
want on your team.

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:23.580
Or even on your species, if we're being honest.

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:28.380
Like Minn-Erva, Att-Lass has had his share
of comics page-time, although to a lesser

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:28.880
extent.

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:33.260
His biggest contribution to the film was getting
mimicked by a Skrull, but there's more to

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:35.180
this blue-faced warrior than meets the eye.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:40.020
In addition to his involvement in Operation:
Galactic Storm, a massive crossover event

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:44.530
that put Earth in the middle of a war being
fought by the Kree and Shi'ar, Att-Lass has

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:47.000
also shown up as the villainous Titanium Man.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:52.730
In the Infinity: Heist miniseries, Att-Lass
becomes Titanium Man to join a crew of villains

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:54.140
robbing Stark Tower.

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:58.690
There, he unmasks himself and reveals that
the Kree were behind the heist the whole time,

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:02.080
causing the rest of the bad guys to turn against
him, and he gets killed in the melee.

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.260
Ah, well… guess not everyone can be a criminal
genius.

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:10.220
Back when Jude Law was announced to be part
of the Captain Marvel cast, most of the internet

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:12.740
speculated he would play the role of Mar-Vell.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:16.260
They were wrong, of course: his real role
is that of Yon-Rogg.

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:20.240
It turns out that Marvel had good reason to
keep Law's role a secret.

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:26.760
See, Yon-Rogg is a well-known enemy of Mar-Vell's,
so fans might have seen the big twist in Captain

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:27.840
Marvel coming a mile away.

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:32.740
In the film, Carol Danvers eventually discovers
that Yon-Rogg killed Mar-Vell at the crash

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:37.660
site of her experimental plane, then abducted
Danvers and took her back to Hala when she

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:39.980
absorbed the power core he'd been after.

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:43.020
That pretty much characterizes the character
in the comics.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:47.560
One detail fans may not have caught about
this whole reveal is that it mirrors a similar

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:50.260
moment in the comics… though with a much
different outcome.

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:56.760
In 1969's Captain Marvel #18, Mar-Vell faces
off against Yon-Rogg, who's kidnapped an Earth

00:10:56.770 --> 00:11:01.610
woman named, that's right, Carol Danvers,
and kept her prisoner near a powerful Kree

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:04.180
machine called the Psyche-Magnitron.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:10.520
After Mar-Vell and Yon-Rogg fight, the Psyche-Magnitron
explodes and seemingly kills Yon-Rogg while

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:14.040
simultaneously knocking some superpowers into
Danvers.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:18.100
In both the comics and cinematic universes,
then, Yon-Rogg is directly responsible for

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:19.600
Danvers gaining her powers.

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:21.680
Maybe he's not such a bad guy after all.

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:23.120
Well, okay, maybe he is.

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:27.520
One member of the Starforce team is much more
recognizable than the others.

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:32.220
Korath the Pursuer, played by Djimon Hounsou,
is no stranger in the MCU.

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:36.580
He first appeared on-screen in the opening
moments Guardians of The Galaxy, where you

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:39.120
may remember he tried to take the Power Stone
from Star-Lord.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:40.800
"Who?"

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:42.980
"Star-Lord… man.

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:43.980
Legendary outlaw...Guys?"

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:46.140
"Move!"

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:52.290
He showed up again on the Battle of Xandar
and proved to be a formidable opponent...at

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:53.400
least until Drax ripped off part of his head.

00:11:54.010 --> 00:11:58.100
With Captain Marvel, we get to see a younger
Korath in his prime, still kicking butt and

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:00.280
still, well, alive and kicking in general.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:04.680
Of all the Starforce members, Korath is certainly
one of the most powerful.

00:12:05.140 --> 00:12:09.490
In the comics, he's a geneticist, and took
to modifying his own body with all kinds of

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:13.140
cybernetic sci-fi implants, giving him super-strength
and speed.

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:17.800
Casual fans may not know that he's gone up
against the likes of Captain America, Scarlet

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:19.300
Witch, and Adam Warlock.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:23.760
Those are big foes, but to be fair, he usually
does come out on the losing side.

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:28.420
Another no-doubt familiar face in Captain
Marvel is that of Ronan the Accuser, who's

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:32.680
name-dropped early in the film before making
his first big appearance during the climax.

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:34.720
You know, he's the blue guy with a big hammer.

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:41.020
Fun fact: That hammer is known as the Universal
Weapon and grants him such abilities as "transmutation

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:43.940
of matter" and "interstellar transportation."

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:47.280
Why don't we ever see any of those abilities
in the films?

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:50.700
Probably for the same reason Ronan's importance
is downplayed in general.

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:55.880
Fortunately, Captain Marvel did offer a hint
of his full power within the Kree empire by

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:58.780
positioning him at the head of a battleship
that goes to Earth.

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:03.800
In the comics, he's one of the most well-known
Kree warriors in a civilization that spans

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:05.180
multiple star systems.

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:09.880
Of course, if we're going to talk about comics
characters whose roles were reduced by film,

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:10.940
we have to mention Mar-Vell.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:16.260
In Captain Marvel, Annette Bening, famous
for roles in films like American Beauty, portrayed

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:19.060
the Kree soldier who was moonlighting as Dr.
Lawson on Earth.

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:24.240
She played an important role in the plot,
but of course there's no mention that Mar-Vell

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:26.460
is, well, the original Captain Marvel.

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:32.000
With his standalone series, launched in 1968,
Mar-Vell's version of Captain Marvel contained

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:35.900
all the elements of the Mar-Vell we saw on
screen, just with superpowers.

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:40.940
He's a Kree warrior who comes to Earth and
disguises himself as Dr. Lawson, then decides

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:43.060
to protect humanity instead of battling them.

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:47.880
In the film, it's the Skrulls Mar-Vell tries
to protect, but it's the same idea.

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:52.200
Unfortunately, the hero wasn't very popular
at the time, and Stan Lee came up with the

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:56.220
idea to deal with him in the least comic-book
friendly way imaginable.

00:13:56.740 --> 00:14:01.260
In the Death of Captain Marvel graphic novel,
Captain Marvel gets cancer and dies.

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:05.760
And, at the end of it all, Thanos visits him
and takes him on a hallucinogenic journey

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:07.760
to fight all his old foes again.

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:09.780
It's weird and strangely heartbreaking.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:15.360
As Yon-Rogg likes to remind us all, the Supreme
Intelligence appears to be something different

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:18.120
to everyone, something important from their
lives.

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:23.160
That's good news for filmgoers, because for
years we've just been seeing the Supreme Intelligence

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:25.340
as a fat, green head floating in a jar.

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:28.120
So let's talk about this Supreme Intelligence
creature.

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:34.180
In the film, we got the idea that it was sort
of a therapist for confused Kree and sometimes

00:14:34.180 --> 00:14:36.140
a prison for people who betrayed the Kree.

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:39.460
The truth is, it is kind of those things in
the comics.

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:44.560
Despite being the Kree leader, it never seems
to have the best in mind for its people.

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:50.440
In Avengers #347, for example, the Supreme
Intelligence detonated a nega-bomb and almost

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:52.260
completely wiped out the Kree.

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:53.660
Whoops!

00:14:54.340 --> 00:14:58.900
Captain Marvel featured a major cameo that
most likely delighted hardcore comics fans.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:04.530
This one came in the train station, when Carol
Danvers is wandering around, lost and still

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:06.040
wearing her Kree uniform.

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:11.460
While multiple people pass her by on the platform,
one lady who walks past is none other than

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:13.400
writer Kelly Sue DeConnick.

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:18.300
DeConnick began writing Captain Marvel in
2012, leading to a version of the character

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:20.640
which heavily inspired the film incarnation.

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:24.620
If you missed her cameo in the film, you probably
weren't alone, but it's definitely one of

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:28.320
those small details you need to keep an eye
out for during any future rewatches.

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:34.240
Finally, it was a bittersweet moment to see
Stan Lee in his final Marvel cameo after his

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:36.680
decades of contributions to the comic book
industry.

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:41.140
Captain Marvel fittingly paid tribute in its
opening moments, switching out the typical

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:44.760
Marvel Studios intro with one dedicated to
the late, great Lee.

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:49.540
Carol Danvers herself gave the man a nod of
respect during his cameo on the train.

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:53.920
That in itself was a fine detail, but the
film went even deeper with the cameo.

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:58.870
Since Captain Marvel is set during the '90s,
Lee appears as himself, reading the script

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:01.240
for Mallrats and trying to remember his lines.

00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:06.520
For those who don't remember, 1995's Mallrats
marked one of Stan Lee's first film cameos.

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:11.320
Of course, it's much more than a cameo, with
Stan Lee giving Jason Lee's character a piece

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:14.540
of sage dating advice through the lens of
comic books.

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:16.520
"Trust me true believer.

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:19.180
Well… good talking to you."

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:20.600
“Keep up the good work.”

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:22.580
"You keep reading them, I'll keep writing
them."

00:16:22.900 --> 00:16:27.680
It has some pretty amazing layers for a cameo
and marks the perfect send-off, looping back

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:31.200
around full-circle to the very beginnings
of Stan Lee's film appearances.

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:32.920
Rest in peace, true believer.

